## Raw outputs

`results/raw/*.pkl` (per-draw predictions, float32) and `results/raw/target_level.parquet` (one row per benchmark, split, n, method and target model) total ~90 MB for the main runs and are regenerated by `run_all.sh`; they are attached to the repository's release assets where the venue permits.

## What this code does not do

Not run: the assessor (Pacchiardi et al.), active-evaluation acquisition (Li et al.), SubLIME, and the original tinyBenchmarks/metabench code; `anchor` (k-means with n_init = 1, 25 iterations, medoid weighted by cluster size) and `infotop` (d = 2 latent-factor Fisher information, top-n) are our implementations of their ideas, not the published code. The estimator grid and hyperparameters were chosen per cell on an inner split of the sources; the headline "best method" rows of the paper are the best of ~100 combinations picked after seeing results, which the tables say.

## Uncertainty, compute, licences (checklist answers)

- **Intervals.** Error bars are 95 % percentile intervals of a family bootstrap: within each benchmark the target model families (individual models for the random-fold split) are resampled with replacement, 2000 replicates, the same replicate for every method of a benchmark so that method-to-method ratios are paired; the aggregate is the geometric mean over benchmarks of the RMSE ratio. They reflect target sampling only: the subsets drawn (R = 10 per fold and budget, 40 in the extrapolation split) are averaged inside the RMSE; benchmarks are treated as fixed, and the benchmarks are not independent (HELM models recur across its benchmarks). Intervals of "best method" rows are optimistic because the method was picked after the results; `results/selected.csv` gives the same classes with the representative chosen without the held-out benchmark.
- **Compute.** CPU only; single-threaded workers (1 CPU, < 6 GB each). Main + sensitivity-split runs 4.7 hours of wall-clock time of single-threaded jobs (an upper bound of CPU time); the remaining stages are listed in `REPRODUCE.md`.
- **Licences.** The score matrices come from a public repository stated to be MIT-licensed (data not redistributed here); the underlying benchmarks' licences were not checked individually. No baseline code was reused: the `benchpred` package of that repository is not used, and every method is implemented in `src/bpred`. The public code of three baselines (tinyBenchmarks, Anchor Points, the py-irt fork) was cloned and run only as an outside check of our re-implementations (`scripts/12_public_baselines.py`, `13_public_compare.py`); none of it is copied into or imported by this repository, and the check is not part of `run_all.sh`'s default stages. No licence is declared for this code here.

